  "title": "Reversible Reactions",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A chemical reaction is usually written in a way that suggests it proceeds in one direction, the direction in which we read. Our convention involves placing reactant formulas on the left side of a reaction arrow and product formulas on the right side, and in most of the reactions we have discussed, the left-to-right reaction occurs essentially to completion, and reaction in the reverse direction is negligible. For example, if hydrogen and oxygen are combined in stoichiometric proportions under certain conditions, they will spontaneously combust to produce water according to:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "uazh9ll2gniyptk",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "jb9fe2zcrcz1r79",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "p9fhysxk6iefu5b",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "2\\;{\\mathrm H}_2(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;2\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The reverse reaction (H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O decomposing to H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ") occurs to such a small extent that it can be ignored. For all practical purposes, we view this reaction as going to completion, so all of the reactants have been converted to products and no appreciable amounts of the reactants remain."
              }
            ],
            "id": "9eq5szewkt18d4c",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The following table describes the progress of this reaction. The table compares:"
              }
