            "type": "table"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "h5ozxnhzmb67e3v",
                "text": "Reversible Reactions"
              }
            ],
            "id": "map88xs85pd97pc",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In some chemical reactions, the reverse reaction occurs to a significant extent and cannot be ignored. Such a reaction is called a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "reversible reaction"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A chemical reaction in which the reactants can convert to products, and the products convert back to reactants, occurring simultaneously in both directions until dynamic equilibrium is reached."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2003347566",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "881305974",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Chemical equilibrium arises when reactions occur in both the forward and reverse directions to an appreciable extent. The decomposition of dinitrogen tetroxide into nitrogen dioxide is an example of such a reaction."
              }
            ],
            "id": "dp4v9qnqjtvr5o9",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "sphtsxvhok0tpm6",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "b39s63z9wnw5h9h",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\underset{\\mathrm{colorless}}{{\\mathrm N}_2{\\mathrm O}_4(g)}\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\underset{\\color[rgb]{0.68, 0.46, 0.12}\\mathrm{brown}}{2\\;{\\mathrm{NO}}_2(g)}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Note the use of the double arrow: "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "src": "<m:math style=\"inline\"><m:mo>⇌</m:mo></m:math>",
                "subtype": "mathml",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ". This symbol denotes a reversible reaction."
              }
            ],
            "id": "y875kolk8cz1jry",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "When a sample of dinitrogen tetroxide (N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ", a colorless gas) is placed in a glass tube, it forms nitrogen dioxide (NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", a brown gas) according to the equation above."
              }
            ],
            "id": "kwx9kaklk37bhqv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Colorless N"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "O"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " reacts to form brown NO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ". As the reaction proceeds toward equilibrium, the color of the mixture darkens due to the increasing concentration of NO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ". Notice that at equilibrium both N"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "O"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and NO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " are present."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "thdl1xjci0l63op",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "height": 299.04998779296875,
            "id": "s00jb75sdjr5eu2",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/95/95e100fa0bf1bf03da9612d1ada6b73e/n2o4_no2_equil.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 521.9097349304112
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In a reversible reaction, the reactants can combine to form products, and the products can react to form the reactants. Thus, not only can N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " decompose to form NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", but the NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " produced can react to form N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ". As soon as the forward reaction produces any NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the reverse reaction begins and NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " starts to react to form N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ". At equilibrium, the concentrations of N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " and NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " no longer change—not because the reaction has stopped occurring but rather because the rate of formation of NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " is exactly equal to the rate of consumption of NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and the rate of formation of N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " is exactly equal to the rate of consumption of N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "r5xka6j92lb7h3d",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In a chemical equilibrium, the forward and reverse reactions do not stop. They continue to occur at the same rate, leading to constant concentrations of the reactants and the products. Following are graphs of how the reaction rates and concentrations change with respect to time."
              }
            ],
            "id": "8wjopyvbdteqr90",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Graphs show "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " concentration versus time and "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " rate versus time for the reaction N"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "O"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " ⇌ 2 NO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "fed4k1d8a2qlgrf",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "8070ywv2gn5mi76",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/c5/c511bad6aa9460b1585061d519376470/n2o4_no2_conc_rate.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The graph on the left shows the changing concentrations of N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " and NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Initially, only N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " exists. This can be observed visually by the fact that the gas is colorless. As N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " begins to decompose and produce NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", its concentration decreases. As more NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " is produced, its concentration increases. At equilibrium, the concentrations of both N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " and NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " remain constant."
              }
            ],
            "id": "m3dq5foscgy3t4t",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The graph on the right shows the reaction rates of the forward and reverse reactions. Initially, only the forward reaction can occur because only N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " is present. As N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " decreases in concentration, the rate of the forward reaction begins to decrease. At first, no reverse reaction occurs because there is no NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ". As soon as NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " is produced, the reverse reaction begins. As the concentration of NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " increases, the rate of the reverse reaction increases. When the rate of the forward reaction is equal to the rate of the reverse reaction, equilibrium is established, and the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant."
              }
            ],
            "id": "5zr57bk6x7tca8t",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The following table describes the progress of this reversible reaction. Starting with 1 mol N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " and no NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", we track the amounts of reactant and products throughout the process."
